 is encrypt some  data in a way that you can safely store  it and retrieve it later but you will  get asked tricky questions like what  block cipher mode do you like is ecb  cool what about cbc hmm difficult  initialization vectors what is one how  do i get one what do i have to do with  it  padding what is padding does it matter  nothing in this api thus far has  reminded you that you might need to  authenticate your data the most  important thing for you to do here and  the thing that the api doesn't ask you a  question about  this has led to a lot of papers being  published pointing out the weaknesses in  many cryptographic libraries with code  that touches cryptography often being  called the most dangerous code in the  world users are expected to be experts  in order to use these libraries  correctly and if anyone in here has  worked with some of the pre-existing  low-level apis like openssl  you may be familiar with what i mean  these difficult-to-use apis have led to  a number of vulnerabilities in all kinds  of products in the software industry and  this is an unacceptable state of affairs  for our industry to be in  as a result there's an important school  of modern cryptographic api design  focusing on misuse resistant apis the  goal is  to provide the user with a limited  number of choices all of which are  secure  in a way that is makes it very hard to  achieve sub-optimal outcomes from the  api if you ask for encryption you should  get the best possible version without  being forced to make too many choices  about what that might mean that could  diminish the value of your result  cryptokit is an important piece of this  design work  it aims to provide these misuse  resistant apis  but  cryptokit is an api that is only  available on apple platforms and here's  the thing none of the problems i just  outlined are specific to developers on  apple platforms  unfriendly difficult to use  cryptographic apis are rife on other  platforms as well  it's important that our entire industry  be working together  to build better safer cryptographic apis  to ensure that as much of our computing  is as secure as possible  the reason is  many applications that run on apple  platforms have components that run on  other platforms they have back-end  servers or web app versions for other  platforms  often these other components need to do  similar work to the client application  they're a critical part of the  cryptography pipeline and they must also  handle the data correctly  to that end i'm thrilled today to be  able to announce our newest tool for  helping developers navigate this subtle  and important space  swift crypto  thank you  swift crypto is a fully open source  re-implementation of the cryptokit apis  that is supported absolutely everywhere  that swift is it is available right now  licensed permissively under the apache 2  license available for swift 5.1 and  later using swift package manager in a  package that is semantically versioned  the package contains two major chunks of  code the first are the definitions of  the public api of cryptokit these files  are the original versions from cryptokit  itself and have been made open source as  part of swift crypto  they are accompanied by a brand new  implementation of those apis that uses  google's boring ssl library  this means there are now two peer  implementations of the cryptokit apis  the first available on apple's platforms  uses apple's proprietary core crypto  library and the second available  everywhere else uses boring ssl  we have implemented every single api in  cryptokit that does not rely on  specialized hardware  that is any api that we can expect to  run on a reasonably wide range of  hardware has been fully implemented  this is the overwhelming majority of the  api surface of cryptokit  so if crypto is available in the swift  package manager without any platform  restriction  when you use it as a dependency on an  apple platform  it delegates to the system