  surprisingly straightforward  this is how you would generate such a  shared secret in cryptokit like the one  i talked about earlier you could pass  such a share you could use such a shared  secret to generate a key for encryption  purposes and you can see that cryptokit  does this in just a few short lines of  code it's shorter than it looks if you  don't try to make the text large enough  to be read from the back  so  what parts of crypto kit does swift  crypto support  i said earlier what doesn't rely on  specialized hardware but to be more  specific essentially everything the only  rule  is that if you see the words secure  enclave that api has not been  implemented in swift crypto it will  continue to function only on apple  platforms that expose a secure enclave  the implementations in swift crypto are  entirely compatible with the ones in  cryptokit  we share the same test suites  we can test the two implementations  against each other  everything we do is also done by  cryptokit  one of the other important areas of  focus for us in this work other than  making sure these two libraries were  perfectly compatible together was to  make sure that we could evolve swift  crypto going forward  with open source projects it's extremely  important that the community is able to  guide the evolution of the project and  to propose new apis to that project as  well as to fix bugs and improve the  infrastructure around the project they  use so i'd like to spend the remaining  time talking a little bit about what our  plans are for evolving this project  the core api of swift crypto everything  that is immediately available  will remain in sync with crypto kits api  as it stands on the latest releases on  apple platforms on which swift crypto is  uh which cryptokit is supported that  means whenever new api is added to  cryptokit we will be adding it to swift  crypto as well except where that api  relies on specialized hardware  of course that covers the case where we  at 